---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/site-layout.astro';

const title = 'S-Corp Reasonable Compensation Guide | LLCAtlas';
const description =
  'Plain-English guide to S-corp reasonable compensation: what it means, why it matters, and how solo founders should think about salary assumptions.';
const pageUrl = 'https://llcatlas.com/s-corp/reasonable-compensation/';
const lastUpdatedIso = '2026-05-22';

const structuredData = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    headline: 'S-Corp Reasonable Compensation',
    description,
    author: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'LLCAtlas',
      url: 'https://llcatlas.com',
    },
    publisher: {
      '@type': 'Organization',
      name: 'LLCAtlas',
      url: 'https://llcatlas.com',
    },
    datePublished: lastUpdatedIso,
    dateModified: lastUpdatedIso,
    mainEntityOfPage: {
      '@type': 'WebPage',
      '@id': pageUrl,
    },
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 1,
        name: 'Home',
        item: 'https://llcatlas.com/',
      },
      {
        '@type': 'ListItem',
        position: 2,
        name: 'Reasonable compensation',
        item: pageUrl,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<SiteLayout title={title} description={description} ogType="article" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-8 lg:py-12">
    <header class="space-y-4">
      <p class="llc-helper">S-corp salary basics</p>
      <h1>Reasonable compensation is the S-corp number you cannot fake.</h1>
      <p class="llc-section-copy text-ink-soft">
        The tax savings from an S-corp come from splitting business profit between owner salary and
        distributions. The salary has to be reasonable for the work performed. A low salary may make
        the calculator look better, but it can make the tax position worse.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="mt-10 llc-card-highlight space-y-5">
      <p class="llc-helper">Practical default</p>
      <h2>Use 50% as a rough screening assumption, not as tax advice.</h2>
      <p class="llc-section-copy text-ink-soft">
        LLCAtlas uses a 50% salary default in the S-corp calculator because it creates a conservative
        first pass for many solo service businesses. Your actual number should consider your role,
        hours, market pay, profit level, and whether the business could hire someone else to do the
        same work.
      </p>
      <a
        href="/s-corp/election-calculator/"
        class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-strong transition hover:bg-accent/85"
      >
        Test salary in the calculator
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 space-y-5">
      <h2>How to think about the salary number</h2>
      <div class="grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <p class="llc-helper">Role</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 font-semibold text-ink-strong">What work do you actually perform?</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            A founder doing client delivery, sales, admin, and operations may need a higher salary
            assumption than a passive owner with employees doing the work.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <p class="llc-helper">Market pay</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 font-semibold text-ink-strong">What would the job cost to replace?</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            Look at comparable pay for the work, then adjust for hours, geography, and company size.
            Do not start from the tax answer you want.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <p class="llc-helper">Profit</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 font-semibold text-ink-strong">Is there enough profit left?</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            If a reasonable salary consumes nearly all profit, the S-corp election may not produce
            meaningful savings after admin costs.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-[1rem] border border-rule px-5 py-5">
          <p class="llc-helper">Documentation</p>
          <h3 class="mt-2 font-semibold text-ink-strong">Can you explain the number later?</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-[15px] leading-7 text-ink-soft">
            Keep the reasoning simple and written down. A CPA can help turn the rough salary
            assumption into a defensible payroll setup.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-12 rounded-[1.5rem] border border-rule bg-surface-subtle p-6 sm:p-8">
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <p class="llc-helper">Related tools</p>
        <h2>Run the full S-corp savings math before you file Form 2553.</h2>
        <p class="llc-section-copy text-ink-soft">
          Salary is only half the decision. Payroll fees, accounting costs, and state annual costs
          can wipe out the savings at lower income levels.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="mt-5 flex flex-col gap-3 sm:flex-row">
        <a
          href="/s-corp/election-calculator/"
          class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-accent px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-strong transition hover:bg-accent/85"
        >
          Run the S-corp calculator
        </a>
        <a
          href="/llc-vs-s-corp/"
          class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center justify-center rounded-full border border-rule-strong bg-surface px-5 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-ink-strong transition hover:border-ink-muted hover:bg-surface-subtle"
        >
          Read LLC vs S-corp
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteLayout>
